window.docContents[148].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___4___-Ideals/Existence-of-maximal-ideals/#Chapter2___Problem2___4___1":{"contents":"Problem 2.4.1. A maximal ideal in a ring A is an ideal I \\neq A such that any strictly larger ideal coincides with A. (This definition is made for left, right, or two-sided ideals.) Show that any unital ring has a maximal left, right, and two-sided ideal. (Hint: Use Zorn's lemma.)\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.4. Ideals\u0009Existence of maximal ideals","header":"Existence of maximal ideals","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___4___-Ideals/Existence-of-maximal-ideals/#Chapter2___Problem2___4___1"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___7___-Examples-of-algebras/Representations-and-ideals-of-the-Weyl-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___7___-Examples-of-algebras--Representations-and-ideals-of-the-Weyl-algebra--Representations-and-ideals-of-the-Weyl-algebra--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"In prime characteristic, the center is generated by the displayed p-th powers.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.7.4; role=primary]\n\nEvery finite-dimensional module of the displayed algebra has zero base-field dimension in characteristic zero.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.7.4; role=primary]\n\nThe displayed algebra is a simple ring over any characteristic-zero field.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.7.4; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.7. Examples of algebras\u0009Representations and ideals of the Weyl algebra\u0009Representations and ideals of the Weyl algebra\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___7___-Examples-of-algebras/Representations-and-ideals-of-the-Weyl-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___7___-Examples-of-algebras--Representations-and-ideals-of-the-Weyl-algebra--Representations-and-ideals-of-the-Weyl-algebra--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Scalar-extension-and-the-Noether-Deuring-theorem/#Chapter3___Problem3___8___4":{"contents":"Problem 3.8.4. (i) Let V, W be finite dimensional representations of an algebra A over a (not necessarily algebraically closed) field K. Let L be a field extension of K. Suppose that V \\otimes_K L is isomorphic to W \\otimes_K L as a module over the L-algebra A \\otimes_K L. Show that V and W are isomorphic as A-modules.\n\nHint: Reduce to the case of finitely generated, then finite extension, of some degree n. Then regard V \\otimes_K L and W \\otimes_K L as\nA-modules, and show that they are isomorphic to V^n and W^n, respectively. Deduce that V^n is isomorphic to W^n, and use the Krull-Schmidt theorem (valid over any field by Problem 3.8.3) to deduce that V is isomorphic to W.\n\n(ii) (The Noether-Deuring theorem) In the setting of (i), suppose that V \\otimes_K L is a direct summand in W \\otimes_K L (i.e., W \\otimes_K L \\cong V \\otimes_K L \\oplus Y, where Y is a module over A \\otimes_K L). Show that V is a direct summand in W.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.8. The Krull-Schmidt theorem\u0009Scalar extension and the Noether-Deuring theorem","header":"Scalar extension and the Noether-Deuring theorem","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Scalar-extension-and-the-Noether-Deuring-theorem/#Chapter3___Problem3___8___4"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___/Classification-of-irreducible-representations-of-S_n-via-Specht-modules-V_lambda/#Chapter5___Theorem5___12___2___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.12. Representations of $S_n$\u0009Classification of irreducible representations of S_n via Specht modules V_lambda\u0009Classification of irreducible representations of S_n via Specht modules V_lambda","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___/Classification-of-irreducible-representations-of-S_n-via-Specht-modules-V_lambda/#Chapter5___Theorem5___12___2___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___17___-The-hook-length-formula/Hook-length-formula___-dim-V_lambda-___-n___-___-product-of-hook-lengths/#Chapter5___Theorem5___17___1___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.17. The hook length formula\u0009Hook length formula: dim V_lambda = n! / product of hook lengths\u0009Hook length formula: dim V_lambda = n! / product of hook lengths","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___17___-The-hook-length-formula/Hook-length-formula___-dim-V_lambda-___-n___-___-product-of-hook-lengths/#Chapter5___Theorem5___17___1___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___1___-Frobenius-Schur-indicator/Nontrivial-finite-groups-of-odd-order-have-non-real-irreducible-representations/#Chapter5___Exercise5___1___7___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.1. Frobenius-Schur indicator\u0009Nontrivial finite groups of odd order have non-real irreducible representations\u0009Nontrivial finite groups of odd order have non-real irreducible representations","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___1___-Frobenius-Schur-indicator/Nontrivial-finite-groups-of-odd-order-have-non-real-irreducible-representations/#Chapter5___Exercise5___1___7___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___25___-Representations-of-___GL_2_LPAR____mathbb___F____q_RPAR____/5___25___4___-Complementary-series/#Chapter5___Discussion_complementary_series_summary___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.25. Representations of $GL_2(\\mathbb{F}_q)$\u00095.25.4. Complementary series\u0009Summary: all q^2-1 irreducible representations of GL_2(F_q) found\u0009Summary: all q^2-1 irreducible representations of GL_2(F_q) found","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___25___-Representations-of-___GL_2_LPAR____mathbb___F____q_RPAR____/5___25___4___-Complementary-series/#Chapter5___Discussion_complementary_series_summary___formalization"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___4___-Roots/Lemma-6___4___6___-Root-coefficients-are-all-nonneg-or-all-nonpos/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___4___-Roots--Lemma-6___4___6___-Root-coefficients-are-all-nonneg-or-all-nonpos--Lemma-6___4___6___-Root-coefficients-are-all-nonneg-or-all-nonpos--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Under the given matrix and vector hypotheses, the integer vector has either only nonnegative coordinates or only nonpositive coordinates.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Lemma6.4.6; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Remark6.4.8; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.4. Roots\u0009Lemma 6.4.6: Root coefficients are all nonneg or all nonpos\u0009Lemma 6.4.6: Root coefficients are all nonneg or all nonpos\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___4___-Roots/Lemma-6___4___6___-Root-coefficients-are-all-nonneg-or-all-nonpos/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___4___-Roots--Lemma-6___4___6___-Root-coefficients-are-all-nonneg-or-all-nonpos--Lemma-6___4___6___-Root-coefficients-are-all-nonneg-or-all-nonpos--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___6___-Adjoint-functors/Section-7___6___-Adjoint-functors/#Chapter7___Introduction_7___6___heading-1":{"contents":"Another fundamental notion in category theory is the notion of adjoint functors.\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.6. Adjoint functors\u0009Section 7.6: Adjoint functors\u0009Section 7.6: Adjoint functors","header":"7.6. Adjoint functors","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___6___-Adjoint-functors/Section-7___6___-Adjoint-functors/#Chapter7___Introduction_7___6___heading-1"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/Categories-without-enough-projectives/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors--Categories-without-enough-projectives--Categories-without-enough-projectives--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"A finite module over a commutative ring of finite type over the integers admits a surjective\nlinear map from a finite free module.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Exercise8.2.9; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00098. Homological algebra\u00098.2. Tor and Ext functors\u0009Categories without enough projectives\u0009Categories without enough projectives\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/Categories-without-enough-projectives/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors--Categories-without-enough-projectives--Categories-without-enough-projectives--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories/Setup-of-the-equivalence-functor-F/#Chapter9___Discussion_before_Theorem9___6___4":{"contents":"Now let \\mathcal{C} be a finite abelian category with a projective generator P. Let B = \\operatorname{End}(P)^{\\mathrm{op}} (a finite dimensional algebra acting on the right on P). Let B\\text{-fmod} denote the category of finite dimensional left B-modules. Consider the functor F : \\mathcal{C} \\to B\\text{-fmod} given by the formula F(M) = \\operatorname{Hom}(P, M).\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.6. Finite abelian categories\u0009Setup of the equivalence functor F","header":"Setup of the equivalence functor F","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories/Setup-of-the-equivalence-functor-F/#Chapter9___Discussion_before_Theorem9___6___4"}});